{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward bias to the forecast path (model is typically ~+1 to +3 EUR/t too low, especially at horizons ~5–20 days).",
    "Trend/level lag: the model reacts too slowly to regime shifts (post-breakout). Apply a faster level adjustment by anchoring early-horizon forecasts closer to the latest spot (increase weight on last observed price vs. model path).",
    "Peak underestimation after upside spikes: following sharp upward moves, the model systematically underestimates the subsequent plateau/secondary highs. Add extra uplift for days 3–15 after an upside spike rather than assuming quick normalization.",
    "Over-optimistic late-horizon in downshifts: when the realized market turns down (or volatility rises), the model’s far-horizon tail stays too high. Impose stronger mean-reversion downward beyond ~20 days if recent returns turn negative.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias shape: apply a hump-shaped correction—largest positive adjustment mid-horizon (≈days 5–20), smaller adjustment near day 1–3, and neutral-to-negative adjustment at the far tail (≈days 20–30) depending on recent trend sign."
  ]
}
